# chip — BOTTOM-UP + BY-DEPTH LAYER CAPS: the chip-scale policy vehicle
# (docs/internal/hier_layer_caps.md §12/§13 Phase 5).  Same design and same
# bottom-up flow as chip_bottomup.buda (3x big2 + 3x mix2 + 100 top buses,
# 432 leaf blocks / 13320 nets, healerless for fast experimentation), with
# ONE extra line declaring the whole policy:
#
#   set_layer_caps_by_depth M3 M5
#
# Counting DEEPEST-FIRST over intrinsic cell levels: the leaf cells (level 1,
# no interconnect of their own) get [lowest..M3], the big2/mix2 cell templates
# (level 2 — this is the band that bites, their cell-local buses) get
# [lowest..M5], and the top cell (level 3, past the argument list) stays
# UNRESTRICTED so the 100 cross-instance top buses keep M6/M7 to themselves.
# That is the BKM the whole feature exists to express, in one line instead of
# a set_cell_layer_cap per cell type.
#
# The separation is visible in report_wirelength's per-layer breakdown: zero
# cell-template WL above M5, and M6/M7 exclusively top-level.  Compare against
# chip_bottomup.buda (identical but uncapped) for the §12 study.
source chip_tracks.buda
open_bdb chip.bdb.sql
set_bottom_up big2
set_bottom_up mix2
align_bottom_up
set_layer_caps_by_depth M3 M5
derive_busterms 2
add_blocks_from_bdb 0
add_blocks_from_bdb 1 skip
add_blocks_from_bdb 2 skip
run_hier_bundler depth 2
generate_hier_topologies
run_planner hier 5 signal_tracks
run_nuts
check_template_tracks on_mismatch independent
run_detailed_nuts
report_wl
check_design
exit
